{{Japanese name|Jōhei}}

'''[[Tadahisa Jōhei]]''' (襄平 忠久, ''Jōhei Tadahisa''; born 2 November 1946) is a Sanese politician and former bureaucrat who served as the 51st [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 2011 to 2012. A long-serving member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), he represented [[Okami 2nd District]] in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] for nearly thirty years.

Although his tenure as Prime Minister lasted only 19 months, Jōhei is considered one of the most consequential leaders in modern Sanese history due to his orchestration of the '''Jutsu Restoration''' ({{nihongo|術の維新|Jutsu no Ishin}}). His administration oversaw the constitutional referendums that restored the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]] to the status of a constitutional monarch and officially reverted the capital's name to [[Okami]], effectively ending the post-war "Federal Republic" transitional era.

His premiership ended abruptly in August 2012 amid a deepening diplomatic crisis with [[Kwangju]] and the revelation of a slush fund scandal within the LDP, paving the way for the brief and tragic administration of [[Sasuke Genba]].

==Early life and education==
Jōhei was born in the Bunkyo ward of [[Okami]] (then known as the Federal Capital District) in 1946. He came from a family of high-ranking civil servants; his father served as the Vice-Minister of Finance during the "Go-go 8090" economic boom. This background immersed Jōhei in the machinery of statecraft from an early age.

He attended the prestigious [[Okami University]], where he studied law. During the student protests of the late 1960s—the aftershocks of the [[Quiet Revolution]]—Jōhei was noted for his non-ideological stance, preferring to focus on administrative law rather than radical student politics. He graduated near the top of his class in 1969.

==Bureaucratic career==
Following graduation, Jōhei entered the [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] (MITI). During the 1970s and 1980s, he gained a reputation as a brilliant but cautious technocrat. He was instrumental in drafting the "Industrial Rationalization" policies of the [[Naoto Emi]] administration, which helped transition Okaiken's economy from heavy manufacturing to high-tech electronics.

He rose to the position of Bureau Chief but resigned in 1989 to enter politics, frustrated by what he saw as the bureaucracy's inability to adapt to the bursting of the economic asset bubble.

==Political career==
Jōhei was first elected to the Diet in the [[Okaiken general election, 1990|1990 general election]], running in the Okami 2nd District. He joined the LDP's moderate faction, the ''Kōchikai'', known for its dovish foreign policy and focus on economic development.

Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, he held various cabinet and party posts, including Chairman of the Policy Research Council and Minister of Justice. In 2008, Prime Minister [[Tarō Asō]] appointed him [[Minister of Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|Minister of Foreign Affairs]]. In this role, Jōhei laid the groundwork for Okaiken's eventual entry into the [[United Sovereign Nations]] (USNW), engaging in months of shuttle diplomacy to secure the support of European and Adonian powers.

===Prime Minister (2011–2012)===
Following the resignation of Prime Minister [[Heitarō Seda]] due to health reasons in January 2011, Jōhei won the LDP leadership election. He campaigned on a platform of "National Identity and Global Integration," arguing that for Okaiken to globalize, it first needed to "remember its soul."

====The Jutsu Restoration====
Jōhei's boldest political gamble was the '''Jutsu Restoration'''. Since the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968 and the 1970 Constitution, the status of the Emperor and the national identity had been legally ambiguous. Jōhei argued this ambiguity weakened the state. In mid-2011, he pushed two controversial referendums through the Diet:

* '''The Capital Restoration Act:''' This successfully reverted the capital's name to its historical pre-war name, "Okami," stripping away the "Federal District" nomenclature imposed during the post-war era.
* '''The Monarchy Act:''' This amended Article 1 of the Constitution. While maintaining the Emperor's lack of political power, it elevated his status from a mere "symbol" to the "Constitutional Monarch and Head of State," satisfying traditionalists while maintaining democratic checks and balances.

The success of these referendums granted Jōhei a temporary surge in popularity, which he used to finalize Okaiken's accession to the USNW.

====The "Summer of Crisis" and Resignation====
By the summer of 2012, Jōhei's political capital had evaporated. The "restoration" of imperial symbols provoked a severe diplomatic backlash from [[Kwangju]] and [[Peilan]], who viewed it as a glorification of the Imperial past. Kwangju conducted naval exercises near Sanese territorial waters in July 2012, and Jōhei's refusal to deploy the Maritime Self-Defense Force in response led to accusations of weakness from the LDP's rising nationalist wing, led by [[Toshiro Kurosawa]].

Simultaneously, the "Seiwakai Slush Fund" scandal broke. Prosecutors revealed that millions of En in unreported political donations had been funneled to senior LDP officials to fund the Restoration referendums. While Jōhei was not personally indicted, his faction was heavily implicated. Facing a revolt from the nationalist wing and plummeting poll numbers, Jōhei announced his resignation on August 10, 2012.

==Legacy==
[[Tadahisa Jōhei]] remains a divisive figure. To conservatives, he is the hero who restored the nation's dignity and cultural heritage. To progressives, he opened a Pandora's box of nationalism that fueled the rise of the far-right in the subsequent decade. Historians generally agree that while his tenure was short, the structural changes of the Jutsu Restoration make him the "architect of the 21st-century Sanese state."

He retired from politics in 2019 and currently serves as the Chancellor of Okami University.

